@meta
id: 11-13
title: Drill — star, cgn, dot
chapter: 11
type: drill
skills: cgn
par: 27
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: Ctrl+D eight times
open: fees.ts

@brief
Rename `amt` to `amount`. Eight occurrences, all of them changing.

Cursor on the word, `*`, `cgn` with the new name, then dot to the bottom.

@keys
| `*` | search for the word under the cursor |
| `cgn` | change the next match |
| `.` | and the next |

@hints
`*` first. If you are typing `amt` into a search you are doing more work
than the file requires.
--
After the first `cgn` there is nothing left to aim. Count the remaining
matches and press `.` that many times.

@solution
    w  *  cgn amount <Esc>   then  .  seven times

Three keys of setup, the new name typed once, and the rest is one finger.

Drill it until `*cgn` is a single gesture. It is the most common rename
you will ever do — a local variable, a parameter, a property that only
lives in this file — and reaching for it should not require deciding
anything. The moment it becomes automatic, you will stop reaching for
find-and-replace on jobs this size, which is a good instinct: this route
shows you every match as it changes and that one does not.

@start fees.ts
const amt = order.total - order.discount;
const fee = amt * FEE_RATE;
const net = amt - fee;
const vat = amt * VAT_RATE;
const gross = amt + vat;
logger.info('fee breakdown', { amt, fee, net, vat });
ledger.debit(order.account, amt);
ledger.credit(FEES_ACCOUNT, amt);

@expected fees.ts
const amount = order.total - order.discount;
const fee = amount * FEE_RATE;
const net = amount - fee;
const vat = amount * VAT_RATE;
const gross = amount + vat;
logger.info('fee breakdown', { amount, fee, net, vat });
ledger.debit(order.account, amount);
ledger.credit(FEES_ACCOUNT, amount);
